{"records":[{"id":"w8d5fn5vxn","title":"Kinetic basis for DNA target specificity of CRISPR-Cas12a. Strohkendl et al.","description":"This dataset includes the raw data used in Strohkendl et al., 2018, and is organized by figure within the publication.\n\nThere are two types of data presented: gel images and fluorescence data. The images either show binding and dissociation of Cas12a and its radiolabeled DNA substrate on native polyacrylamide gels or cleavage of a radiolabeled DNA substrate by Cas12a on denaturing polyacrylamide gels. The fluorescence data reports on 2-aminopurine fluorescence increase as a function of R-loop formation.\n\nBoth types of data are used to determine the rates binding, cleavage, and dissociation of Cas12a while targeting a short DNA duplex.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-19T15:01:24.138Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w8d5fn5vxn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w8d5fn5vxn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Isabel","last_name":"Strohkendl","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Enzyme Kinetics"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"st7hsjzfw6","title":"PQQ","description":"This data is for article “Pyrroloquinoline quinone production with methanol as substrate by Hyphomicrobium sp. CGX1 screened based on high-throughput fluorescence-activated cell sorting”","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-18T07:36:56.045Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/st7hsjzfw6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/st7hsjzfw6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daoyang","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Fermentation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"67sjx5y6xc","title":"Data for: Preparation 2-(anthracen-9-yl)-1,3-dithiolane as a novel dual-channel AIE-active fluorescent probe for mercury (II) ion with excellent performance","description":"A novel fluorescent probe molecule, 2-(anthracen-9-yl)-1,3-dithiolane (AADT), is designed and synthesized for Hg2+ detection successfully, based on a good aggregation induced emission (AIE) fluorescent mechanism and a specific reaction type. DMF-H2O mixed solvent (Fw 80% V/V) is suitable to disperse AADT for forming 1×10-5 mol·L-1 solution with an AIE-active fluorescence probe system. With the increase of trace Hg (II), the AADT probe shows dual-channel, (turn-off at 429 nm, and turn–on at 511nm) fluorescence probe character. (R2>0.994), which does not interfere each other. The probe demonstrates excellent selectivity, anti-inference properties, and several metal ions and acid ions hardly affect its performance. The LOD of the AADT probe is lower than 2.34×10-8 mol·L-1. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-04-28T12:25:28.968Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/67sjx5y6xc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/67sjx5y6xc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jie","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Cuihong","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Weiguo","last_name":"Zheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qingyang","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuping","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yao","last_name":"Xiao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manman","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Applied Sciences","Heavy Metal","Fluorescence","Detection Technique","Probe"],"journal_issn":["10106030"]},{"id":"9nsk2tcjsz","title":"AutoSpectral: mouse brain imaging panel","description":"A mouse brain sample stained with a 10-colour flow panel for sorting microglia, oligodendrocytes and astrocytes on the FACSDiscover S8.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-24T23:14:07.264Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9nsk2tcjsz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9nsk2tcjsz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Oliver","last_name":"Burton","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adrian","last_name":"Liston","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05f950310","name":"KU Leuven","grant_id":"C1 program"},{"id":"https://ror.org/029chgv08","name":"Wellcome Trust","grant_id":"222442/A/21/Z"},{"id":"https://ror.org/0472cxd90","name":"European Research Council","grant_id":"874707 (EXIMIOUS)"},{"id":"https://ror.org/001aqnf71","name":"UK Research and Innovation","grant_id":"Proactive Vaccinology Award MR/Y004450/1 (IMMPROVE)"}],"categories":["Flow Cytometry","Spectral Unmixing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gphwtzz36y","title":"CReCOM Microglia","description":"A Novel Microglia-Speciﬁc Transcriptional Signature Correlates With Behavioral Deﬁcits in Neuropsychiatric Lupus, Frontiers in Immunology, Feb 2020. Neuropsychiatric symptoms of systemic lupus erythematosus (NP-SLE) affect over one-half of SLE patients, yet underlying mechanisms remain largely unknown. We isolated microglia from young and aged SLE-prone mice (CReCOM, CD11cC8). We demonstrate that CReCOM mice develop NP-SLE, including behavioral deﬁcits prior to systemic autoimmunity, reduced brain volumes, decreased vascular integrity, and brain-inﬁltrating leukocytes. NP-SLE microglia exhibit numerical expansion, increased synaptic uptake, and a more metabolically active phenotype. Microglia from multiple SLE-prone models express a “NP-SLE signature” unrelated to type I interferon. Rather, the signature is associated with lipid metabolism, scavenger receptor activity and downregulation of inﬂammatory and chemotaxis processes, suggesting a more regulatory, anti-inﬂammatory proﬁle. NP-SLE microglia also express genes associated with disease-associated microglia (DAM), a subset of microglia thought to be instrumental in neurodegenerative diseases. Further, expression of “NP-SLE” and “DAM” signatures correlate with the severity of behavioral deﬁcits in young SLE-prone mice prior to overt systemic disease. Our data are the ﬁrst to demonstrate the predictive value of our newly identiﬁed microglia-speciﬁc “NP-SLE” and “DAM” signatures as a surrogate for NP-SLE clinical outcomes and suggests that microglia-intrinsic defects precede contributions from systemic SLE for neuropsychiatric manifestations.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-06T21:53:36.625Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gphwtzz36y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gphwtzz36y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Carla","last_name":"Cuda","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microglia","Systemic Lupus Erythematosus","Neuropsychiatric Disorder","Mouse Model"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8j2gsn3bwv","title":"CReCOM Brain Macrophages","description":"A Novel Microglia-Speciﬁc Transcriptional Signature Correlates With Behavioral Deﬁcits in Neuropsychiatric Lupus, Frontiers in Immunology, Feb 2020. Neuropsychiatric symptoms of systemic lupus erythematosus (NP-SLE) affect over one-half of SLE patients, yet underlying mechanisms remain largely unknown. We isolated brain-infiltrating macrophages from aged SLE-prone mice (CReCOM, CD11cC8). We demonstrate that CReCOM mice develop NP-SLE, including behavioral deﬁcits prior to systemic autoimmunity, reduced brain volumes, decreased vascular integrity, and brain-inﬁltrating leukocytes. NP-SLE microglia exhibit numerical expansion, increased synaptic uptake, and a more metabolically active phenotype. Microglia from multiple SLE-prone models express a “NP-SLE signature” unrelated to type I interferon. Rather, the signature is associated with lipid metabolism, scavenger receptor activity and downregulation of inﬂammatory and chemotaxis processes, suggesting a more regulatory, anti-inﬂammatory proﬁle. NP-SLE microglia also express genes associated with disease-associated microglia (DAM), a subset of microglia thought to be instrumental in neurodegenerative diseases. Further, expression of “NP-SLE” and “DAM” signatures correlate with the severity of behavioral deﬁcits in young SLE-prone mice prior to overt systemic disease. Our data are the ﬁrst to demonstrate the predictive value of our newly identiﬁed microglia-speciﬁc “NP-SLE” and “DAM” signatures as a surrogate for NP-SLE clinical outcomes and suggests that microglia-intrinsic defects precede contributions from systemic SLE for neuropsychiatric manifestations.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-06T21:51:39.063Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8j2gsn3bwv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8j2gsn3bwv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Carla","last_name":"Cuda","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Brain","Systemic Lupus Erythematosus","Macrophage","Mouse Model"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"448vt24b4z","title":"Electroacupuncture alleviates neuroinflammation by regulating microglia polarization via STAT6/PPARγ in ischemic stroke rats","description":"Our previous study showed that electroacupuncture (EA) intervention produced a protective effect on cerebral ischemia-reperfusion injury (CIRI) in rats and may be related to the anti-inflammatory effects of microglia. This study aimed to investigate further whether EA could modulate neuroinflammation by targeting the STAT6/PPARγ pathway, a key regulator of microglia. Middle cerebral artery occlusion (MCAO) model rats were used, and 6 h after reperfusion, EA interventions were performed in Chize (LU 5), Hegu (LI 4), Sanyinjiao (SP 6), and Zusanli (ST 36) on the affected side of the rats, the group that received EA in combination with the STAT6 phosphorylation inhibitor AS1517499 was used as a parallel control. The degree of neurological impairment, infarct volume, microglia polarization, inflammation levels and activity changes in STAT6/PPARγ pathway were then assessed by neurological deficit score, TTC staining, immunofluorescence, western blotting, and quantitative real-time PCR. The data showed that EA intervention significantly alleviated nerve injury, reduced infarct volume, enhanced STAT6/PPARγ pathway activity, inhibited NF-κB activity, increased the number of M2 microglia and the release of associated anti-inflammatory factors, and inhibited microglia M1-type polarization and expression of pro-inflammatory factors. In contrast, inhibition of STAT6 phosphorylation exacerbated neural damage, inhibited STAT6/PPARγ pathway activity, promoted microglia M1-type polarization and exacerbated neuroinflammation, resulting in an attenuated positive effect of EA intervention on the regulation of microglia-associated neuroinflammation in MCAO rats. Therefore, we concluded that EA intervention could attenuate microglia-associated neuroinflammation by enhancing the activity of STAT6/PPARγ pathway, thereby reducing CIRI in MCAO rats.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-08T06:56:43.147Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/448vt24b4z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/448vt24b4z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zengyu","last_name":"Yao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Li","last_name":"Cai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aimei","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhiyu","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guiming","last_name":"Liang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Meng","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiuhong","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guoping","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microglia","Acupuncture","Brain Ischemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7764dnh2pf","title":"Raw data on the protective effects of conditioned media from IL-1alpha and CoCl₂-primed human mesenchymal stem cells on stressed microglia.","description":"This dataset contains the raw ELISA and ICC measurements from a study designed to evaluate the protective capacity of conditioned media (CM) derived from human bone marrow-derived mesenchymal stem cells (hBMSCs). To enhance their secretory profile, hBMSCs were primed with either the pro-inflammatory cytokine Interleukin-1 alpha or the hypoxia-mimetic agent cobalt chloride (CoCl₂). The protective effects of the resultant CM were then assessed on a microglia cell line subjected to two distinct stress paradigms: lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced inflammation and an in vitro model of ischemic stroke (oxygen-glucose deprivation and reperfusion, OGD/R). The ELISA data are provided as concentrations (in pg/mL) using equations derived from 4-parameter fit absorbance-concentration curve. ICC values are provided as corrected total cell fluorescence (CTCF). CTCFs are determined by multiplying the area of selected cells by mean fluorescence of background and subtracting the result from the integrated density of the selected cells.  ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-01T16:17:48.206Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7764dnh2pf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7764dnh2pf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maryam","last_name":"Salaudeen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroscience","Pharmacology","Ischemia","Brain Ischemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hngpdgb82z","title":"Hydroxytyrosol reduces BPS-induced neuroinflammation by regulating microglia polarization through inhibiting oxidative stress by inactivating of NF-κB signaling pathway","description":"This study mainly studied the toxic effect of BPS on microglia, and induced oxidative stress by activating NF-κB signaling pathway to polarize microglia towards M1, inhibit M2 polarization and promote inflammation, thus causing damage to the central nervous system; while hydroxytyrosol treatment reversed the effects of BPS, polarized microglia towards M2, inhibited inflammation. Protects the central nervous system from neuroinflammation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-24T09:11:18.701Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hngpdgb82z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hngpdgb82z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zhang","last_name":"ZH","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yun","last_name":"Hou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guoshun","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Toxicology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y7crpxfvcf","title":"Fluorescence micrographs demonstrating the distribution of inherently fluorescent Janus kinase 3 inhibitors","description":"The dataset contains supplementary images for the publication \"Inherent fluorescence demonstrates immunotropic properties for novel Janus kinase 3 inhibitors\". In short, cells were incubated with fluorescent JAK3 inhibitors or control fluorophore 13 (appearing in green) as well as stains for lysosomes (appearing in red) or nuclei (appearing in blue). For in vivo samples (tail blood and organs), C57/BL6 mice were treated p.o. with the aforementioned JAK3 inhibitors or the control. Again, nuclei were stained blue and compounds appear in green.\nFile names indicate the test compound used (1-13), the sample type (cell type or organ), incubation time (for in vitro samples), sampling time post treatment (for in vivo tail blood samples) and the level of magnification. \"Overlay\" indicates a merge of channels for compound fluorescence, lysosome and nucleus stains. \"Overview\" indicates a merge of singular captures to give an overview of the entire organ sample.\nHBC = human buffy coat\nHMC3 = human microglia cells\nPBMCs = peripheral blood mononucleated cells\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-21T06:49:25.172Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y7crpxfvcf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y7crpxfvcf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Laux","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariella","last_name":"Martorelli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Simon","last_name":"Straß","orcid":"0000-0003-3803-6976"},{"first_name":"Florian","last_name":"Maier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Burnet","orcid":"0000-0003-4311-5441"},{"first_name":"Stefan","last_name":"Laufer","orcid":"0000-0001-6952-1486"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lysosomes","Fluorescence Microscopy","Compound Uptake","Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":105557,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":122,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":105557,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":105202,"restricted":62,"embargoed":293},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1687},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":515},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":404},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":384},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":361},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":352},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":312},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":244},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":198},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":193}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":103144},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1786},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":165},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":104},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":63},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":62},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":54},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":32},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":29},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":390},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":380},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":349},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":272},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":270}]}}